Sculpt and rig the **Caldera Colossus**, the massive elite bruiser of the Slag — a
ponderous, columnar obsidian quadruped on four thick column legs, its enormous body
carrying an open furnace crater in its back that exposes a molten acid-green core — as a
{{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}} opaque-voxel model using only the
`voxel-anim` tool. You build it by **painting opaque cells** one operation at a time,
and you **author its move, attack, and aura animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief tells
you *what the Colossus is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how you break
the creature into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape every
form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a ponderous, crushing
creature needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and the
ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json` —
together with the rig you define are your submission.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each
a parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the required
animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation targets a
**part** with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it; each part is
sculpted **separately** in its own preview and log. The volume starts **empty**. Voxels
are **opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency. Each call **records only** —
it appends the operation to that part's log and renders nothing; run `voxel-anim render`
when you want to (re)draw the per-part previews (`parts/<part>.png`) and the assembled
scene.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name body
voxel-anim fill-box --part body --x0 1 --y0 1 --z0 1 --x1 5 --y1 4 --z1 5 --color "#241f2b"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands (defining parts,
setting pivots, defining joints, and defining/keyframing animations), and `voxel-anim
<operation> --help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the
authoritative contract. **Run `voxel-anim render`, then read `parts/<part>.png`** to see
that part's progress **and the assembled-scene previews under `scene/`
(`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`, `scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole
Colossus composed from all your parts, drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene
views are how you confirm the parts actually fit together: that each column leg seats
under its own corner of the enormous body, the shoulder guards sit over the front
legs, and the open furnace crater is set cleanly into the back with the molten core
exposed inside it. Because each part is sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates,
position each part where it belongs on the finished Colossus; the scene simply composes
them there.

