## What to deliver

Build and rig the Colossus up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png` matches
the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together correctly, and
the rig is complete: a massive, columnar obsidian quadruped on four thick column legs, an
enormous body with heavy shoulder guards, an open furnace crater set into its back
exposing a molten acid-green core with molten seams radiating from the crater across the
body, and a contiguous accent region on the guards and the crater rim sculpted **only**
in `#4a4358` and appearing nowhere else on the model. **Author the three required
animations** as F-curves with the `voxel-anim` animation subcommands: a **`move`** (a
ponderous, immensely heavy walk whose column legs land with weight, authored in place), an
**`attack`** (a crushing slam that rears and brings the full mass down, then settles, while
the feet stay planted), and a self-playing **`aura`** (an idle whose furnace core pulses
and breathes and whose molten seams swell and dim with it, moving no leg). The animations
and the accent region are the fixed contract — produce all of them; the parts and joints
that realize them are yours. You may add your own extra parts, joints, or animations on
top, but do not drop or contradict the required `move`, `attack`, and `aura`. Before you
finish, run `voxel-anim render` so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry your result is
built from — an unrendered part is scored as empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>`
redraws one part; `voxel-anim render --time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model
posed at that instant of an animation to check the motion). When you are satisfied, stop:
the recorded per-part logs and `rig.json` are your finished submission.
